  "Id": 1690,
  "Title": "At the cutting edge of breast cancer research ",
  "NewsSubTitle": null,
  "NewsSummary": "Faculty investigate the exciting potential of testosterone in fighting breast cancer.",
  "GlobalNews": true,
  "AlternateNewsTitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSubtitle": null,
  "AlternateNewsSummary": null,
  "NewsContent": {
    "type": "doc",
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Faculty members are pioneering a new field in breast cancer research that could lead to treatments that are both more effective and more affordable than those currently available. "
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Researchers led by Professor El-Nasir Lalani - Director of AKU's Centre for Regenerative Medicine and Stem Cell Research - are exploring the capacity of androgens to protect against breast cancer and to fight various subtypes of breast cancer. They have found that women with breast cancers that are androgen-receptor positive have enhanced survival times of up to 10 years."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The researchers' experimental work on breast cancer cell lines has demonstrated that testosterone significantly inhibits the growth of breast cancer cells driven by an onco-protein called HER-2. This inhibitory effect is as effective as utilizing Herceptin, a drug for HER-2-positive breast cancers that costs tens of thousands of dollars for a full treatment course. "
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "The results are especially notable because treatments utilizing testosterone alone would be significantly cheaper than Herceptin - as would utilizing Herceptin in combination with testosterone, because it would lower the required Herceptin dose. "
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Assistant Professor Nazia Riaz, a breast surgeon and member of Professor Lalani's team, is extending the study to other breast cancer subtypes and is seeking funds to undertake a clinical trial to determine if the laboratory findings can be reproduced in a clinical setting. "
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "\"Our aim is that the research we undertake results in the development of affordable and optimal treatments for patients with breast cancers and other cancers,\" she said."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "hard_break"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  },
  "PullQuote": "AKU's research could impact the diagnosis and treatment of patients with breast cancer not only in Pakistan but globally.",
  "PullQuoteAuthor": "Professor El-Nasir Lalani ",
